# Transient chaos runs: exploratory matrices, partial scenario sweeps, anything
# aimed here by `--out artifacts/chaos/`. These are working output and must stay
# ignored, or a half-finished sweep can be committed as if it were the result.
artifacts/chaos/
# The *published* kill matrix is a deliberate exception and lives in its own
# directory so it needs no `git add -f` and cannot be produced by accident:
# `--out artifacts/chaos-matrix/` is the documented publishing command, and that
# path is intentionally NOT ignored. Keeping the two apart is what lets the
# README lead with a committed, reproducible table.

# Local planning and multi-session handoff notes. These are working documents,
# not project documentation, and they record local paths and scheduling context.
IMMEDIATE-PROJECT-OPSPILOT.md
PROJECT-HANDOFF.md
# The handoff's companions: design decisions measured against the private spec,
# findings, the MCP position, and decisions awaiting the owner. They quote the
# spec and carry local planning context, so they stay working documents. Much of
# their content belongs in public docs eventually -- the route for that is the
# packaging chunk lifting it into docs/, not un-ignoring these.
NOTES-*.md
# ...and here is that route taken. `docs/NOTES-TO-REVIEWER.md` is the published
# map of where to be skeptical, written for whoever clones this repository rather
# than for one person mid-review. It matches the pattern above by name only. The
# negation is explicit because the pattern would otherwise drop it silently from
# `git add -A`, leaving `docs/HANDOFF.md` linking to a file no clone contains.
!docs/NOTES-TO-REVIEWER.md
# A reviewer's own status and baseline guide for one pass over the repository:
# what has landed, what is next, which baseline a number should be read against.
# A working document by the same test as the two above -- written for one person
# mid-review, not for a reader of this project.
#
# Ignored for a second, concrete reason as well. `chaos.matrix` stamps `dirty`
# from `git status --porcelain` scoped to everything outside
# artifacts/chaos-matrix/, so an untracked note at the repository root makes the
# published kill matrix stamp `dirty: true` and stop being reproducible from its
# own revision. A review note should not be able to decide that.
#
# Whether any of this becomes project documentation is the owner's call, and the
# route for that is lifting it into docs/, not un-ignoring it here.
REVIEW-*.md
# Exactly one historical working-notes audit, not the docs/audits/ directory:
# honest audit documents are content this project should publish, so a future
# docs/audits/*.md must be committable without editing this file.
docs/audits/2026-08-11-r09-r12-wip-audit.md

# Local agent/editor workspaces.
.claude/worktrees/
